      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-i/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Suppose a, b are integers satisfying the equation. [M1] [A1] Then a2 = 8b + 7, [A1] so the right side is odd: Hence a must be odd; write a = 2n + 1 for an integer n. Then (2n + 1)2 = 8b + 7, 4n2 + 4n + 1 = 8b + 7, 4n2 + 4n −8b = 6, 2n2 + 2n −4b = 3, 2(n2 + n −2b) = 3, n2 + n −2b = 3/2. An integer cannot equal 3/2. This is the source’s contradiction: In modular form the original equation gives a2 ≡3 (mod 4). [M1] The square of an even integer is 0 (mod 4), and the square of an odd integer is 1 (mod 4). [A1] This contradiction rules out all integer solutions . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Prove by the method of contradiction that there are no integers a and b which satisfy the following equation. 2 − = ( ) 6 a 8 b 7"
