    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-i/q11-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The source substitutes u = sin 2x first, so dx = du/(2 cos 2x); the endpoints x = 0, π/4 give u = 0, 1. Convert tangent and secant to sine and cosine: Writing s = sin x, c = cos x, this gives Z 1 1 −s2/c2 I = 1 (1/c2 + 2s/c)(c2 −s2) du 2 0 Z 1 c2 −s2 Z 1 du = 1 (1 + 2sc)(c2 −s2) du = 1 1 + u. 2 2 0 0 The cancellation holds in the interior and the transformed integrand has a finite endpoint value. Equivalently, simplify the original integrand first: Multiply numerator and denominator by cos2 x. [B1] [A1] The quotient becomes cos2 x −sin2 x cos 2x 1 + 2 sin x cos x = 1 + sin 2x. [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-i/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"With u = sin 2x, du = 2 cos 2x dx [A1] and the limits are 0 and 1. [A1] The integral is 1 2[ln(1 + u)]1 0 = 1 2(ln 2 −ln 1) . [M1] [A1] = 1 2 ln 2\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
